[Music]  welcome back in an exclusive interview  Boeing's chief executive tells CBS News  he's sorry about two deadly crashes  involving Boeing 737 max airliner Dennis  Muhlenberg says the company is learning  from the accidents and vows to make  improvements that includes updating  software on the planes that he says will  make the 737 max among the safest  airplanes ever to fly newly announced  CBS Evening News anchor Norah O'Donnell  spoke to Muhlenberg Nora good morning  welcome back thank you did you know how  to find the room I did know how to find  you like yeah this was a fascinating  interview Muhlenberg is taking  responsibility man knows he has work to  do in regaining public trust the Boeing  737 max has been grounded around the  world since March of this year following  those crashes in Ethiopia and Indonesia  that killed 346 passengers and crew when  you heard that a second plane had  crashed the same type of plane what went  through your mind this gets to the core  of who we are as a company you know I've  been at Boeing for 34 years and I've  spent a career working on safe products  we paused we assess the situation we  immediately began to take actions on  what we could do to improve and in the  end this reinforces our values as a  company do you think it would be  appropriate to apologize personally  apologize to some of the families yeah I  do personally apologize to the families  I can tell you it affects me directly as  a leader of this company it's very  difficult now so you know we can't  change what happened in these accidents  but we can be absolutely resolute in  what we're going to do on safety you put  your family on a 737 max without any  hesitation absolutely I've been up on  two of the flight tests myself with the  new software and I would in a heartbeat  put my family on max  what do you say to someone in the flying  public who says I am not comfortable  getting on a Boeing 737 max we know it  will take some time to earn and riyer  n-- that public confidence and the first  step will be to get the max back up and  flying safely flight 610 that crashed  into the Java Sea about 12 minutes after  takeoff killed a hundred and eighty-nine  people on board what's your  understanding of what brought that  flight down we know that there was  inaccurate sensor data that came into  the airplane that there appears to have  been a maintenance issue with that  sensor we know that the EM cast software  was activated multiple times during that  flight it was more than multiple times  it was two dozen times according to the  data released in this preliminary crash  report the pilots were essentially in a  tug of war with the plane and that plane  essentially did a death dive into the  ocean at 450 miles an hour can you  imagine how terrifying that was for the  people on board we examine every  dimension of these accidents not to try  to attribute fault or or point fingers  but it's to understand and and what  happened so that in some cases we can  make changes to the airplane design to  make it safer both of these planes took  a nosedive because of that erroneous  flight sensor information and if one of  the sensors is off it's my understanding  that there could be a warning light but  that light was supposed to be active on  all 737 max Jets and it was not you knew  this in 2017 and you did not tell the  FAA for 13 months why the the  implementation of that software we did  not do it correctly  our engineers discovered that so you're  fixing that now we're fixing it now and  in our communication on that was not  what it should have been  does Boeing have a credibility and  transparency problem if they don't admit  what were the mistakes in the past we  clearly fell short and and the  implementation of this angle-of-attack  disagree alert was a mistake in the  weeks  after the first crash there were pilots  who met with one of your vice presidents  Mike sunette a lot of pilots who said we  didn't know about this and caste system  yeah yeah inset they blamed Boeing yeah  and we respect we should respect that  input  I'll never shy away from from pilot  commentary in fact I prefer that hard  candid feedback I think it's good it's  good for us it makes us a better company  and in the end it makes for safety in  flight that's pretty strong Nora how  does Boeing's bottom line do we think  factor into his admission now that  they've made mistakes well he says look  safety is their number-one priority  that's a value of the company but it's  clear that it's costing this company a  lot of money 737 is its most profitable  and one of its most popular planes and  one of things he said they're gonna be a  hundred and fifty million new customers  in Asia every year flying flying  customers and they like that market  likes the 737 Boeing is a great American  company right it leads the Dow many  times which is the Dow Ford so but  they're doing everything they've they  finished this software fix that we  talked about they submitted it to the  FAA they're waiting for the FAA approval  it could come as soon as this month that  those planes are back up the air but I  think what you heard him say it  acknowledged for the first time a change  in tone is that this has damaged public  trust confidence in Florida that the  issue to that issue do you think Boeing  would would even consider rebranding  this plane effectively well as you know  the president Trump suggested that in a  tweet that and so I asked Muhlenberg  about this and he said this is not a  rebranding issue this is a safety issue  so no plans to rebrand the plane I think  it's good that he spoke up everybody  that knows him says that he's a really  good CEO but I was curious about his  demeanor Nora off camera because on  camera he seems so clinical to me and  dare I say a tad distant considering the  pain that this accident these two  accidents have caused can you tell us  anything about him  I mean he is you know dyed-in-the-wool  kind of Boeing employee he started he's  an ayat from an Iowa farm boy you know  who started off as an intern and 34  years later made his way up to CEO so I  think he cares deeply about that he's a  he's been walking the halls but you can  he's a big-time CEO they do a lot of  defense work so he is buttoned up and so  there was a change in tone in him  talking about personally apologizing to  these families he says it's been the  most challenging part of his entire  career I appreciate him talking to you  yeah or interviews like that right here  at the table no nor walked in the room  and said I'm back I have something for  you two on Monday always good to see you  you can hear more of Nora's interview  with Boeing CEO Dennis Muhlenberg on  today's CBS this morning podcast

